                  "doc_id": "RSE-RW-01",
                  "title": "Remote & Hybrid Work Policy",
                  "section": "6.1",
                  "section_heading": "What counts",
                  "text": "Temporary remote work means working for a period from somewhere other than your usual work location, such as a family home or a holiday rental, while doing your normal job. Travelling to do Ridgeline work at a client, a project site or another Ridgeline office is business travel, not temporary remote work; see RSE-LOC-01 \u00a72.",
                  "score": 1.0
                },
                {
                  "doc_id": "RSE-RW-01",
                  "title": "Remote & Hybrid Work Policy",
                  "section": "6.2",
                  "section_heading": "Within your state or province",
                  "text": "Temporary remote work within the state or province of your usual work location is allowed. Tell your manager where you'll be working.\nNo approval is needed, but you must still meet core hours and, if you are office-hybrid, plan around your anchor days. For example, an Austin employee spending a week working from family in another Texas city just tells their manager.",
                  "score": 1.0
                },
                {
                  "doc_id": "RSE-RW-01",
                  "title": "Remote & Hybrid Work Policy",
                  "section": "6.3",
                  "section_heading": "Another US state or province",
                  "text": "Temporary remote work from another US state, or another province, needs your manager's approval for up to 30 calendar days per calendar year in total. Anything over 30 calendar days in a year needs HR review: your manager can't approve it alone, and HR decides.\nThe 30 calendar days count every day you are away, including weekends, and they add up across all trips in the calendar year. For example, a stay from the first of one month to the tenth of the following month is well over 30 calendar days, so it needs HR review even if your manager supports it. Submit the request in the HR Portal with your dates and location; HR reviews longer stays because working in another state or province can affect payroll and tax registration.",
                  "score": 1.0
                },
                {
                  "doc_id": "RSE-RW-01",
                  "title": "Remote & Hybrid Work Policy",
                  "section": "6.4",
                  "section_heading": "Another country",
                  "text": "Working from another country always needs HR review, whatever the length. See RSE-LOC-01 \u00a73 for the rules and the Cross-Border Review.\nYour manager can support the request, but cannot approve it. Plan well ahead: the review takes time, and there are notice and security requirements before you go.",
                  "score": 1.0
                },
                {
                  "doc_id": "RSE-RW-01",
                  "title": "Remote & Hybrid Work Policy",
                  "section": "6.5",
                  "section_heading": "Field/site roles",
                  "text": "Field/site-based employees are not eligible for temporary remote work.\nTheir work depends on being at the project site. Travel to another site for work is business travel (RSE-LOC-01 \u00a72).",
